{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1795359222,"nonce":"apawaIbKZgaSo1HYV0q/O8uE3xQ=","title":"Cranelift loops cannot promote a function that has OSR entries: loops_hot refuses on bound.generation() != 0","message":"hxn reported that the NaGap hl.Bytes loop (rb) was left at 1.68 ns per element for a whole run in 1 of 54 hybrid runs, against 0.10 normally. The same hybrid binary run 250 times one at a time never did it; six runs at a time gave 2 of 360 and 1 of 800.\n\nThe tier log of a slow run shows NaGap.rb getting Cranelift code and the demand line \"shape=caller-loop\", then nothing: no OSR transfer for rb, no LLVM compile, no install. In a normal run rb is promoted because its first interpreted activation transfers through OSR and calls force_llvm_for_live_frame. When that does not happen, nothing else asks for the top tier: rb is called from a compiled closure, which ticks nothing, and the interpreter's own count never reaches the tier-1 threshold.\n\nThe mechanism meant for this is loops_hot (tiering.rs): Cranelift loops call it every loop_threshold iterations, and it proposes tier 1. A temporary log in it showed rb's call firing over ten thousand times in the slow run, and every call refused. The first took the early return on `bound.generation() != 0`; the one-shot flag loops_proposed had already been set before that check, so every later call skipped.\n\nbound.generation() is not a promotion count. A re-swap that attaches OSR entries also bumps it. A function whose loops the interpreter probed has OSR entries, so it has generation 1 as soon as its Cranelift code is installed, and loops_hot never proposes it. The guard was copied from queue_llvm_promotion, where it means \"compiled-only baseline\". loops_hot only works for a function whose Cranelift code was installed without OSR entries (the pidigits helpers in loop_owner_promotion.rs).\n\nReproducer: crates/ash/test/tests/TestLoopsHot.hx. A kernel with a 1024-iteration loop is called straight-line from interpreted code, with 100 iterations per call so its loop is probed, then only through a compiled caller. With --jit-log the log has \"[loops] generation 1\" and the kernel never gets an LLVM install, in every run.\n\nFix: drop the generation guard (llvm_done and the adapter's queued flag already stop a second promote), and set loops_proposed only once the function is known to be eligible, so a missing bound does not latch it.\n\nNot covered: hxn's 0.1.21 observation of `--mode jit` giving NativeArray 1.70 once in two runs. That mode has no interpreter, so this is not the same path, and it is not reproduced.","files":null}]}